---
import {
  Alert02Icon,
  Analytics01Icon,
  Calendar03Icon,
  FileSearchIcon,
} from '@hugeicons/core-free-icons'
import FeaturePage from '@/components/FeaturePage.astro'

const related = [
  { title: 'seo report', text: 'The overview and each section, from quick wins to traffic drops.', href: '/docs/report', icon: Analytics01Icon },
  { title: 'seo page', text: 'One URL: live page facts, Google index status and its queries.', href: '/docs/page', icon: FileSearchIcon },
  { title: 'seo compare', text: 'Before and after a date, with the Google updates in the window.', href: '/docs/compare', icon: Calendar03Icon },
  { title: 'seo index', text: 'Pages that lost their clicks, checked live and with URL Inspection.', href: '/docs/indexing', icon: Alert02Icon },
]
---

<FeaturePage
  title="Google Search Console MCP for AI agents"
  description="Connect Google Search Console to Claude, Codex or any AI agent with a free MCP server. Read clicks, impressions, CTR, positions, queries, pages and URL Inspection."
  path="/features/search-console"
  lead="Ask your agent about your own search performance. It reads your clicks, impressions, CTR, positions, queries and pages straight from Search Console."
  {related}
>
  <h2>Connect Search Console in one step</h2>
  <p>
    Add the hosted MCP to your agent and sign in with Google. Access is read
    only, and you need to be able to see at least one Search Console property.
    Your search data is never stored: the server fetches what your agent asks
    for and returns it. Prefer to keep everything local? The CLI reads the
    same data from your machine.
  </p>

  <h2>What your agent can ask Search Console</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>What runs</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>What is going on in search?</td><td>The <a href="/docs/report">SEO report</a> overview: totals against the previous period by device, with the top pages and queries.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Where are the quick wins?</td><td>Report sections for quick wins, low CTR, striking distance, second page, content decay, cannibalization and traffic drops.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>What is happening with this page?</td><td><a href="/docs/page">seo page</a>: live page facts, URL Inspection status and the page's queries.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Did the release or the update change anything?</td><td><a href="/docs/compare">seo compare</a>: equal periods before and after a date, with Google updates in the window.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Which pages fell out of search?</td><td><a href="/docs/indexing">seo index</a>: pages that lost their clicks, checked live and with URL Inspection.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Anything else?</td><td><a href="/docs/data">seo data</a>: raw rows by query, page, date, country, device or search appearance.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Bing beside Google</h2>
  <p>
    Connect Bing Webmaster Tools and your agent sees Bing's clicks and
    impressions next to Google's for the same dates, with each engine's share
    of your search traffic. Bing runs in the <a href="/docs/bing">CLI</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Facts with their limits visible</h2>
  <p>
    Search Console hides some rare queries and reports average positions, and
    recent days are not final. The reports use the latest final days and say
    when a section is empty or capped, rather than turning missing rows into a
    zero. A Google update that overlaps a drop is reported as timing, not as
    the cause.
  </p>
</FeaturePage>
